From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 display engine -- DRAM into TCON_TV1

[Recipe cut: the debugfs files enable, fill, layer, ccsc and background are
not carried; the datapath is driven by the KMS master only. What follows on
configs/a733_defconfig, scripts/ and the checkpatch line count describes the
development tree this patch was written in.]

The block that reads pixels out of memory. `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` gave this SoC a raster and
`drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream` gave it a DisplayPort stream, and the picture on the monitor today comes
out of TCON_TV1's own VDBIST pattern generator with no framebuffer anywhere in
the path. TV_SRC_CTL_REG bit 0 has exactly two settings --
the DE provides the data source (0), or the VDBIST test-pattern generator
does (1) -- so this patch writes the other source.

WHAT IT DOES, AND WHAT IT LEAVES AT RESET
------------------------------------------

One display pipe, one VI channel, one layer, XRGB8888, the 1920x1200 mode in
sun60i-display.h that the TCON and the transmitter already share. The driver
allocates its own DMA-coherent framebuffer and paints it, so the fetch path is
provable with no KMS device above it -- the same shape `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` used to prove a
TCON before there was a CRTC to hang it on. Enable, fill, background and a
register dump are debugfs files; nothing happens at bind.

Left at reset DELIBERATELY, and named so nobody wonders whether they were
overlooked: scaling (beyond bypass), colour management, the 17x17x17 LUT,
HDR/cdc tone mapping, smart backlight, dynamic gamma and de-banding, AFBC/TFBD
compressed sources, write-back, offline composition, online rotation, 10-bit
and YUV paths, the other six channels, the other three layers of this channel,
and the second display output. The capture measures why that is cheap: of
29 233 non-zero words on a live link, 26 315 (90 %) sit on 31 pages that are
essentially solid coefficient and LUT data. ELEVEN REGISTERS CARRY THE
FRAMEBUFFER -- six on the channel, five on the blender -- and ten of the eleven
are computed from the mode. Same shape as the TCON result: fifteen registers
behind a working link.

THE MANUAL DOES NOT DOCUMENT THIS BLOCK
----------------------------------------

A733 UM V0.92 chapter 14.3 "Display Engine" is two pages of prose.
No register list, no offset table, no field table.
Measured over the whole 2.7 MB UM extract and re-counted independently:

  GLB_CTL   0 hits      blender / Blender   0 hits
  BLD_      0 hits      RCQ                 0 hits (the queue appears as ONE
  OVL_      0 hits                            feature bullet)
  mixer     1 hit, and it is in 14.5 describing G2D's input geometry

Compare TCON_TV, which gets its own register chapter with per-bit field tables
(15.2.10), or eDP (15.4.7). The DE gets neither, which is a known gap in the
manual.

SO NO DE REGISTER IN THIS PATCH CARRIES A MANUAL REFERENCE, and asking for one is asking
for a fabrication. The evidence floor here is one level lower than anywhere
else in this tree: a live capture, plus mainline's own model of this DE
generation. Every value says which of three things it is.

