The four blender instances at a 0x400 stride are ALL written identically, and
that is CARRIED: the UM lists two display outputs, so "one per lit
output" is not what four register files are, and the live link holds all four
identical on a board with exactly one pipe up. A working link's demonstrated
state, not a theory about what the other three are for.

THE ONE THING NOT UNDERSTOOD IN THE CONTROL PLANE. DE2TCON_MUX's disp0 nibble
is 5. This SoC has four TCONs (LCD0, LCD1, TV0, TV1), so a
plain index would make TCON_TV1 = 3. Two readings survive: a global index in a
numbering space wider than this part populates, or an enable bit plus an index
([3:1] = 2 with bit 0 set). This patch writes the word a working link carries
and says it does not know why it is that word. Falsified by: writing 0x0000fff3
or 0x0000ffff on a live link and watching the screen -- one command, and it
settles the encoding.

ON THE BOARD: THE GATE FIX WORKS, AND THE SCREEN IS BLACK FOR A DIFFERENT REASON
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

✅ ONE `echo 1 > enable`, NOTHING TYPED AFTER IT, and the whole datapath is
programmed by the driver: the three de_top enables read 0x00010001 / 0x00010001
/ 0x00000011, the layer reads ATTCTL 0xff008403, MBSIZE and OVL_SIZE 0x04af077f,
PITCH 0x00001e00, LADDR0 0xfe400000, and the blender reads PIPE_CTL 0x00000101,
FCOLOR0 0xff000000, INSIZE0 and OUTSIZE 0x04af077f. No "NOT OPEN" error. The
aperture fix and sun60i_de_verify() both did their job.

🔴 AND THE SCREEN IS BLACK. TCON source handed over (TV_SRC_CTL = 0x00000000),
TCON SCANNING to 1234 of VTOTAL 1235, underflow AND lostwarn both clear during
the window, framebuffer verified red by reading DRAM back
(0xFE400000 = 0x00FF0000), scanout address unchanged, monitor receiving signal
and not in standby. `bld0 ROUTE` written by hand to 0x00543210: no change.
`layer` toggled: no change. Module clock moved DEPLL3X 696 MHz -> PERI0_480M
480 MHz: no change. And all ~21 registers logged from the run that DID produce a
picture are bit-identical -- so the difference is outside what that log covered.

🔴 THE DERIVED CANDIDATE, AND THIS PATCH IMPLEMENTS IT: THE CCSC MATRIX
------------------------------------------------------------------------

A systematic diff against the vendor's live link -- 74 registers read at exactly
the addresses its capture holds non-zero in the pixel-path blocks --
shows the vendor setting
24 words we leave at zero: three per CCSC instance, at +0x10, +0x24 and +0x38,
all reading 0x00020000, in all eight instances.

THOSE THREE OFFSETS ARE THE DIAGONAL OF A 3x3 MATRIX. Under a 3-row x 4-word
row-major layout -- three coefficients and one constant per row, rows on a 0x10
stride from +0x10 -- they are exactly c00, c11 and c22:

    c00 = 0x10 + 0*0x10 + 0*4 = +0x10   ✓ non-zero in the capture
    c11 = 0x10 + 1*0x10 + 1*4 = +0x24   ✓ non-zero in the capture
    c22 = 0x10 + 2*0x10 + 2*4 = +0x38   ✓ non-zero in the capture

...and the NINE off-diagonal words that layout predicts are all zero in the same
capture. Three hits out of three with nine matching zeros, on a block whose name
says a 3x3 matrix is what it holds. That is a UNITY MATRIX and 0x00020000 is its
"1". The page's word count agrees independently: 32 non-zero words on 0x100000 =
8 x (1 control + 3 diagonal).

WHY IT EXPLAINS THIS BLACK SCREEN AND THE OTHER CANDIDATES DO NOT. This driver
wrote CCSC_CTL = 1 -- ENABLING the block -- and left the matrix at its cold
reset value, which is zero. AN ENABLED COLOUR-SPACE CONVERTER WITH AN ALL-ZERO
MATRIX OUTPUTS ZERO FOR EVERY PIXEL. Every symptom follows: data still flows, so
the TCON never underflows and never sets lostwarn; the layer, blender, sizes and
scanout address all read back correct; the framebuffer really does contain red;
and no clock change can help, because the pixels are being multiplied by zero.

⚠ AND IT RETIRES THE ARGUMENT THAT PUT THE 1 THERE. This patch reasoned that
eight instances reading 1 on the vendor's link made 1 the RESET DEFAULT. The
vendor also writes 24 coefficient words, so it CONFIGURES this block rather than
leaving it alone -- which makes 1 the vendor's value, not the reset, and makes
the A523/T527 series' ctl = 0 the more likely of the two readings after all.

SO BOTH ARE REACHABLE FROM debugfs `ccsc`, and one board session separates them
instead of two: 1 (the default) writes the vendor's configuration, enabled with
the unity matrix; 0 disables the block the way the prior art does. The three
coefficients are in `state`. HYPOTHESIS, NOT PROOF -- nothing here has been on a
board.

⬜ AND THE REST OF THE DIFF IS ANALYSED, NOT GUESSED AT, AS FOLLOWS: one entry
struck (0x05008104 is mainline's read-only GLOBAL_STATUS -- a symptom, never a
cause, and not writable), two entries needing a read-twice test before they are
compared once (0x05008098 looks like a counter, not a configuration), and the
remaining four ranked into a bisect order. ⚠ NOTHING IN THAT LIST IS PROVEN TO
MATTER: all 30 deltas were applied in one batch, the board wedged, and the
attribution was lost. This patch therefore changes exactly ONE thing, so the
next run is a single-variable test.

