🔴 THE DEFECT: THREE de_top WORDS OPEN THE APERTURE, AND THIS DRIVER LEFT THEM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    0x05008000 = 0x00010001     first cut wrote nothing -- left 0x00000000
    0x05008004 = 0x00010001     first cut wrote nothing -- left 0x00000000
    0x05008008 = 0x00000011     first cut wrote nothing -- left 0x00000000

Without them the channel window at 0x0510xxxx and the blender at 0x0528xxxx DO
NOT ACCEPT A WRITE AT ALL. Not "fail to latch" -- they read back zero exactly
like unmapped space, while de_top at 0x05008xxx answers normally. Proven
causally at the register level with this driver out of the picture entirely,
by raw devmem:

    before:  devmem 0x05101000 32 0xFF008403 ; devmem 0x05101000 -> 0x00000000
             (0x05200000, genuinely unmapped, also reads 0x00000000)
    after the three writes:
             devmem 0x05101000 32 0xFF008403 ; devmem 0x05101000 -> 0xFF008403
             devmem 0x05281000 32 0x00000101 ; devmem 0x05281000 -> 0x00000101

HOW THIS PATCH GOT IT WRONG, because the shape of the mistake is the reusable
part. An earlier analysis of the vendor capture listed all three as UNKNOWN,
and this driver read UNKNOWN as "leave at reset". But that same analysis shows them
NON-ZERO ON A WORKING LINK -- which is exactly the evidence that somebody writes
them. A word a live link holds and a cold block does not is a WRITE, not a
mystery, and "not understood" is not a reason to omit it; it is a reason to
carry it and say so. All three are now written and tagged CARRIED, with the bit
shape (0x00010001 is this SoC's de-assert-reset + gate pattern) as the only
INFERRED part.

⚠ AND THE SECOND, ORDERING HALF OF THE SAME DEFECT -- STATED AS AN INVARIANT,
because the next person to touch this driver's reset needs the rule and not the
anecdote:

    RE-APPLY AFTER EVERY reset_control_deassert(), BEFORE ANY DATAPATH WRITE.

Two of the three are the DE's INNER reset+gate registers, and RST_BUS_DE0
(DE0_BGR bit 16, this node's own reset) CLEARS THEM along with everything else
inside the block. Measured: after an `enable 0` / `enable 1` cycle they read
0x00000000 again, because sun60i_de_clocks_off() asserts exactly that reset. So
they are not a probe-time one-shot and sun60i_de_top_open() MUST NOT be moved to
probe or to anywhere else that runs once. Anything that touches this driver's
reset owns re-running it afterwards.

Supplied by hand AFTER enable on the board they let `layer` program the channel
while the blender stayed at zero, because enable had already written it into a
closed window -- which is the same rule seen from the failing side.

⚠ THE FAILURE WAS SILENT, AND THAT IS NOW FIXED SEPARATELY FROM THE CAUSE. The
first cut printed "scanning 1920x1200 XRGB8888 from ..." beside a `state` dump
that was an honest column of zeros: a statement of intent next to the evidence
against it. Two changes, and both outlive this bug:

  - sun60i_de_verify() reads back ONE register per datapath window after the
    commit and REFUSES rather than announcing success. Its two outcomes are
    different faults and it says which: reading zero means the window is not
    open, and it prints 0x05008000 / 0x05008004 / 0x05008008 with their expected
    values; reading something else means the window is open but did not take the
    write, which is the shadow the RCQ exists for and is not a content problem.
  - `state` now lists the three ENABLE words FIRST, with their wanted values, so
    a zero is visible next to everything it explains.

⬜ ONE OBSERVATION NOT TURNED INTO A CHANGE. The board's final state carries
bld0 ROUTE = 0x00543210 where this driver writes 0. Whether that is the cold
reset value or one of the hand writes that completed the blender is NOT
established, so nothing is concluded from it. Both encode pipe 0 <- channel 0 in
the only nibble that is read while one pipe is enabled, and 0 is what the
vendor's live link carries. Falsified by: a picture that needs the identity
value.

THE QUESTION THAT SHAPED THIS DRIVER -- MEASURED, AND THE METHOD IS THE FINDING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The v35x DE has a register command queue: a DMA engine that walks 16-byte
descriptors in DRAM and copies register blocks into the DE on each frame-start
pulse from the bound TCON. The vendor's live link has one ARMED -- head array
at DRAM 0xffff3fc0, RCQ_HEAD_LEN 0x760 = 118 x 16 exactly, and that the length
divides by the descriptor size to an integer is what turns "a plausible pointer
and a plausible length" into a structure. The A523/T527 series for this same DE
version states flatly that the datapath latches ONLY through that queue and
that plain CPU stores land in a shadow which never latches. If that held here,
every datapath register above would be the right CONTENT delivered the wrong
WAY, and this patch would be a DMA pool and a descriptor discipline instead.

IT DOES NOT HOLD. Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19, on a fresh vendor boot with
the vendor's driver resident and its DE live -- IN THIS ORDER:

  0. THE OBSERVER PROVEN FIRST: the monitor confirmed BY EYE to be showing the
     console shell. Not assumed, established before any write.
  1. devmem 0x05101018 32 0xFC400000   (+4 MiB) -- the register holds, and the
     SCREEN GOES BLACK, monitor still on and NOT in standby.
  2. devmem 0x05101018 32 0xFC000000   -- THE SHELL COMES BACK.

Three states on one register, the return leg included, with the observer's
state established up front. A PLAIN MMIO WRITE TO THE VI LAYER'S SCANOUT
ADDRESS REACHES THE DATAPATH, and the RCQ is not mandatory for it.

⚠ STEP 0 IS NOT CEREMONY -- IT IS THE ENTIRE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A RESULT AND A
COINCIDENCE, AND THIS PROJECT PAID FOR THAT LESSON. A first run of exactly
this sequence produced exactly this outcome and was WITHDRAWN, because nobody
had established that the person watching had the monitor switched on. A black
screen from a monitor that is off is indistinguishable from a black screen the
write caused. Same commands, same answer, and only the second run is evidence.
Nothing was salvaged from the first; it was deleted and redone.

That discipline is carried into the acceptance sequence below rather than left
as a moral: step 6 (`fill`) writes a colour into the framebuffer and touches NO
register, so it proves the observer AND the fetch path in one command -- and it
runs BEFORE step 7 moves a register. The two must never be done in the other
order.

TWO LIMITS SURVIVE THE RESULT, and they are why the commit path stays an
indirection rather than being inlined:

  1. it proves the VI LAYER'S SCANOUT ADDRESS latches. It does not prove every
     DE register does. If some other block's write appears not to take effect,
     the RCQ is back on the table FOR THAT BLOCK.
  2. the write was made while the VENDOR DRIVER held the device -- queue armed,
     datapath already loaded. A COLD-PROGRAMMED DE, whose queue was never armed,
     is what this patch produces and it is untested. ⬜ ITS FALSIFIER, and it is
     step 7 of the acceptance run: all eleven registers written, every one
     reading back correctly, screen black.

THE COMMIT ABSTRACTION, AND WHAT THE OTHER IMPLEMENTATION WOULD COST
---------------------------------------------------------------------

struct sun60i_de_commit is three ops -- begin, stage, arm -- and direct MMIO is
the REAL implementation, not a placeholder. The split it draws is not this
driver's invention: de_top and the mixer top latch directly under every reading
of this hardware, and the blender and the channel are the ones the queue exists
for. So the control plane is plain writel() and only the datapath goes through
the ops. An RCQ backend would stage (offset, value) pairs into a coherent block,
build a head array, and arm the queue in arm() -- and not one register value in
this file would move.

WHAT THAT WOULD COST, from the prior art's own history rather than a guess: its
first cut of sun55i_de.c is ~39 KB and is followed by roughly 200 KB of patches
whose titles are almost entirely RCQ failure modes -- page-flip tearing with
the queue armed, full reload after modeset, a pool out of low CMA, holding old
framebuffers until the arm, block retry with two arms per stage, closing the
races with the arm, channel quiesce before stopping, fixing wedge. The register
content is the easy half; the commit machinery is where the difficulty is.
This patch pays none of it, because MMIO is measured to reach the datapath --
and it stays built so that if some block turns out to need the queue after all,
that costs one backend and no register value.

The driver also DISARMS the queue before touching the datapath -- length to
zero first, so no walk can start from a valid length and a half-written
pointer. On a cold DE those are four no-op stores. They are there for the path
that is not intended: an RCQ left armed by a bootloader or a previous kernel
would reload the datapath from stale DRAM once per frame, silently.

🔴 THE VENDOR RUNS THIS PATH THROUGH AN IOMMU, AND WE DO NOT
-------------------------------------------------------------

The second way this driver can produce a black screen with every register
reading back perfectly, and it has nothing to do with the register set.

MEASURED on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19 with the DE live: 5000000.de sits in
iommu_group 0 alongside the VE and the deinterlacer; sunxi-iommu-v2 at
3900000.iommu comes up with "Default domain type: Translated" and a strict TLB
invalidation policy; and the vendor DT gives de@5000000
`iommus = <&iommu 8 1>` -- MASTER ID 8.

THE PROOF THAT IT IS A TRANSLATION AND NOT AN IDENTITY MAP is direct, and it is
the reason this section exists rather than being a note: 0x05101018 reads
0xFC000000 with a picture on the screen, and a PHYSICAL read at 0xFC000000
returns 0x005E0001 / 0xFE40FB00 / 0x005E0001 at +0/+4/+0x1000 -- structured
data, not pixels. System RAM is 0x40000000-0x23FFFFFFF, so the address is in
range; it simply is not where the framebuffer physically lives.

THREE CONSEQUENCES.

  1. ⛔ SCANOUT ADDRESSES IN A VENDOR CAPTURE ARE NOT COMPARABLE TO OURS. Every
     other register in this patch can be diffed against that capture. This one
     cannot: 0xFC000000 there is an IOVA, and whatever this driver programs is
     not. Anyone "fixing" a mismatch in SRC_DMA_ADDR against the capture is
     chasing a difference that is supposed to be there.

  2. ⚠ OUR TREE HAS NO IOMMU SUPPORT AT ALL. No patch mentions one, no DT node
     exists, no `iommus` property is anywhere in it -- so on our kernel the DE's
     address reaches the bus UNTRANSLATED. THAT IS CORRECT IF AND ONLY IF THE
     IOMMU IS GENUINELY IN BYPASS OR OFF FOR US, AND NOTHING HAS VERIFIED THAT.
     It is named here as the risk it is, not assumed away. If the IOMMU comes up
     translating with no mapping for master 8, the DE fetches from nowhere.
     Mainline 6.18.36 does ship drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c; whether it matches
     this SoC's sunxi-iommu-v2 is UNVERIFIED and this patch assumes neither way.

  3. ✅ THE DRIVER IS ALREADY WRITTEN FOR EITHER WORLD, and that is why it uses
     the DMA API rather than a hand-computed address. dma_alloc_coherent() hands
     back a dma_addr_t, which is a physical address today and becomes an IOVA
     automatically on the day an IOMMU node and an `iommus` property exist --
     with NO change in this file. A hand-rolled physical address would be
     silently wrong on that day.

     And it does not merely assume which world it is in: it asks
     device_iommu_mapped() and SAYS, at enable and in `state`:

       scanout buffer at 0x........ -- a physical address, no IOMMU is attached
       scanout buffer at 0x........ -- an IOVA, this device is behind an IOMMU

     One line, and it turns "we think the IOMMU is off" into a recorded fact of
     the board session.

⛔ WHAT THIS PATCH DOES **NOT** ASK THE DEVICE TREE FOR: an `iommus` property.
Adding one speculatively is worse than leaving it out -- a phandle to an IOMMU
node with no driver bound leaves this device deferring forever, and the symptom
is a driver that never probes. It arrives together with a VERIFIED IOMMU driver
for this SoC or not at all. If that day comes, this driver needs no change; the
DMA API does it.

Mainline 6.18.36 already models this display-engine generation:
SUN8I_MIXER_DE33 in drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun8i_mixer.h:157-161, compatible
"allwinner,sun50i-h616-de33-mixer-0" at sun8i_mixer.c:859, with the channel
formula map[channel] * 0x20000 + 0x1000. Our silicon decodes under it register
for register, including mainline's own unexplained 0x03010301. What this patch
takes from it is the DECODING KEY -- field layouts, bit positions, the size
macro, the format number -- re-derived against our capture and credited above,
value by value. Not one line of code is copied and no mainline file is touched.

The intended direction is for the DE to eventually
join sun4i-drm as a sunxi_engine backend, and that stands. This
patch does not do it yet, for reasons that are about testability rather than
taste:

  - sun4i_drv.c is a component master driven by a display-engine node with
    allwinner,pipelines, and sun4i_tcon_bind() returns -EPROBE_DEFER without a
    matching mixer AND display-engine (sun4i_tcon.c:1121-1125). So the smallest
    working unit there is the whole KMS stack: CRTC, encoder, connector, DP
    bridge and fbdev at once. If it did not light up, nothing would say whether
    the eleven registers were wrong or the plumbing was.
  - sun4i_crtc_init() takes a struct sun4i_tcon *, so reusing that CRTC means
    either folding `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` into sun4i_tcon.c -- throwing away a HW proof to win a
    quirk fight over TV_BASIC1, BASIC0, a second reg window and a closed schema
    -- or refactoring shared mainline code that seven other SoCs use, where we
    cannot see a regression.
  - sun8i_mixer_cfg.map[] is sized 6 and a full A733 needs 7.
  - the device tree for any of that belongs to a separate patch.

So: prove the register set first, in isolation, with a driver shaped like the
one that proved the TCON. The move into sun4i-drm is then a change of frame
around a known-good register set, not a change of frame AND an unknown register
set at the same time. ⓘ Revisit as soon as this is HW-proven -- that is the
right moment for that move.

THE TWO TRAPS THE REGISTER MAP RECORDS, AND WHAT THIS DRIVER DOES WITH THEM
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Neither is ignored and neither is guessed at. All three words are READ on a
cold DE before anything is written and reported by debugfs `state`, which is
also the first instalment of the DE reset-state dump this tree does not have.

1. THE PER-CHANNEL SCALER (VSU, slot + 0x4000) is not a "leave it" item. The
   prior art reports the v35x channel datapath as ALWAYS overlay -> VSU ->
   blender with no bypass, and that after power-gating and re-ungating the DE
   the filter-coefficient SRAM powers up holding RANDOM data; its stated reason
   the vendor never hits this is that Android never power-gates the DE. Our
   capture cannot settle it, because the vendor stack never power-gated it
   either -- and this driver does, on every enable.
   WHAT IT DOES: writes VSU_CTL = 0, which is what a working link carries, and
   does NOT invent a unity coefficient table. The VSU register map is prior art
   only, unconfirmed at this offset on this part, and writing dozens of
   unverified words into a block in the fetch path is the guess this tree does
   not make.
   FALSIFIED BY: a picture that is present but visibly corrupt -- the series
   describes it collapsing to a repeated column. That reading turns a
   six-register channel setup into one that also loads coefficients.

2. THE CHANNEL CSC (slot + 0x800) reads 0x00000001 -- enabled -- in eight
   instances at a 0x100 stride, on a board where exactly ONE layer of ONE
   channel was fetching. That eight-of-eight pattern is the argument that 1 is
   this block's reset default rather than a vendor configuration.
   WHAT IT DOES: writes 1, the value a link with correct colour carries. The
   A523 series writes 0 here for a linear-RGB layer; that is code, on a
   different part, and it disagrees with our own silicon on this part. Silicon
   beats code.
   FALSIFIED BY: wrong colour -- channels swapped or washed out -- and `state`'s
   cold CCSC line then says which way to go. The fix is one line.

3. THE TILED-FRAMEBUFFER DECODER (TFBD, slot + 0x5400) reads 0x00002103 in four
   instances at a 0x100 stride -- and it reads that on SLOT 6, a channel that
   was fetching nothing, which is the argument that it is an idle value and not
   a configuration.
   WHAT IT DOES: reads and reports it, and does not write it. The series warns
   that a stale TFBD "mangles the fetch" and clears it, but its register map is
   unconfirmed here and our framebuffer is linear -- and zeroing an unknown
   block in the fetch path to satisfy a comment about another part is exactly
   the defect class of trusting another SoC's register map.
   FALSIFIED BY: a fetch that produces tiled or garbage output while every
   register reads back correctly.

TWO REGISTERS ARE WRITTEN THAT THE LIVE LINK LEAVES AT ZERO, and both are
departures this patch owns rather than discovers later. PREMULTIPLY = 0 is the
value either way and is written so a bootloader cannot leave it set for a
format with no alpha. BKCOLOR = opaque black is what mainline initialises and
the vendor does not; it is invisible behind a full-screen opaque layer, which
is exactly why the vendor can do without writing it, and it is the first thing
that shows the moment a plane is smaller than the screen -- the out-of-tree
series added a patch titled "black blender background" for that reason.

ONE REGISTER IS DELIBERATELY NOT WRITTEN where mainline would. BLD_OUTCTL, at
+0xfc, is mainline's interlace bit -- but the A523 block map puts +0xa0..+0xff
in the colour-key region, not the control region, so the two candidate names
conflict and our capture cannot choose: it reads zero, which is correct under
both. This mode is progressive, so nothing needs it, and a write into a
register whose identity is contested is not made.

WHERE THE PRIOR ART DIFFERS, AND WHAT THIS DRIVER DOES INSTEAD
-------------------------------------------------------------

Two bodies of code are in scope and neither is ported --
both are decoding keys -- so this is what would have come across had they been.

| the A523/T527 v35x series | ours |
|---|---|
| reuses mainline's H616 channel map {0, 6, 7, 8} unchanged for a part it does not describe | slots {0,1,2,6,7,8,9} measured on OUR silicon: seven scaler pages on an exact 0x20000 stride, against the UM's own seven blending channels. Three VI, four UI |
| puts the layer address at +0x10, because it drives a UI channel | +0x18, because slot 0 is a VI channel with three plane pitches and three plane addresses. Mixing the two silently points the scanout at what is a FILL COLOUR register in the other layout -- both maps are internally consistent, which is what makes it dangerous |
| writes the channel CSC control to 0 for a linear-RGB layer | 1, which is what A733 silicon carries while producing correct colour, with the cold value reported so the first run says whether 1 was even a change |
| clears TFBD unconditionally on a register map it hardcodes for another SoC | read and reported; the block is not written on evidence we do not have |
| its first cut is followed by ~200 KB of RCQ failure-mode fixes | not paid, because MMIO is measured to reach the datapath here -- with the observer proven first, which is why that measurement stands. The seam that would let us pay it for one block is three function pointers |

| the vendor DRM (191 026 LOC, ~21 % hardware-essential) | ours |
|---|---|
| DRM_SUNXI_PQ_PROC: a custom tuning ioctl | no custom ioctl. Tuning is a property, a debugfs file, or it does not exist |
| cdc (HDR tone mapping, 55 617 LOC), smbl (smart backlight, 25 083) and an unbuilt panic screen with a bitmap font (17 560): 98 260 lines, 51 % of the tree, none of which this driver needs | none of it |
| 757 KB of PQ tuning tables in .data against 487 KB of .text for all executable code | no tables |
| needs .atomic_precheck, a drm_plane_helper_funcs hook that does not exist upstream | no core patch; the same checks fit in the existing atomic_check callback |
| its port notes claim DRM_OBJECT_MAX_PROPERTY must go 24 -> 64 | already 64 in 6.18.36 (drm_mode_object.h:63). Nothing to port |

AND WHERE THE COMPARISON IS NOT EARNED, PLAINLY: the A523 series has a page
flip, vblank, plane state, scaler coefficients, format conversion and a working
KMS device. This patch has none of those. It is one static layer brought up
from debugfs. Its advantage over that series is the evidence per value and the
measurement that removes the RCQ from the critical path -- not coverage.

THE DEVICE TREE THIS CONSUMES -- AND IT IS ANOTHER PATCH'S NODE, UNCHANGED
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The DE clocks and the DE node come from separate patches in this series (``clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset``/``arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge``), so
this patch contains no DTS and no CCU. It consumes what those patches describe,
byte for byte:

    mixer0: mixer@5100000 {
            compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-de-mixer-0";
            reg = <0x0 0x05100000 0x0 0x00180000>,
                  <0x0 0x05008100 0x0 0x00000040>,
                  <0x0 0x05280000 0x0 0x00020000>,
                  <0x0 0x05008000 0x0 0x00000100>;
            reg-names = "layers", "top", "display", "de-top";
            interrupts = <GIC_SPI 50 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
            clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_DE0>, <&ccu CLK_DE0>;
            clock-names = "bus", "mod";
            resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_DE0>;
            assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_DE0>;
            assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_480M>;
            ports { ... port@1 -> tcon_tv1 ... };
    };

An earlier cut of this patch asked for a node of its own -- one window over the
whole 4 MiB aperture and a compatible of its own. THAT WAS THE WRONG SHAPE and
it is deleted rather than argued for. Two nodes for one block is a worse device
tree, and the node above is already the shape a sun8i_mixer backend will want:
mainline's three window names, plus "de-top" for the DE-global page mainline's
model has no window for and this SoC needs, because DE2TCON_MUX lives there.

SO THE COMPATIBLE IS CLAIMED DELIBERATELY. This driver is a stand-in for a
sun8i_mixer entry, not a rival to one. When that entry lands, ONE patch moves
the string from this driver's of_match_table into sun8i_mixer_of_table and the
device tree does not move at all. A compatible of our own would have made the
handover a DT change as well.

⚠ AND THE WINDOW SPLIT IS CHECKED AT PROBE, WITH THE ADDRESS IN THE MESSAGE.
Every offset in the driver counts from the DE aperture base (0x05000000), which
is what the capture, the register map and the RCQ's own `reg_offset` word all
use; sun60i_de_map() is the single place the four windows exist. Probe requires
each named window to land on the offset the driver expects and to be at least
as long as the driver reaches -- every minimum computed from the code, not
picked. That check is not ceremony: a window at the wrong address on this SoC
does not fault and does not fail to map, it reads zeros or somebody else's
registers, so without it a DT mistake costs a board session instead of a line
in dmesg.

What it looks up and NOTHING else: the four reg windows by name, "bus" and
"mod", and one reset UNNAMED (index 0, so the node may carry reset-names or
not). Three properties in that node are deliberately not read here and are
correct to have: `interrupts` (INTID 82 = GIC_SPI 50 per the A733 user manual; nothing
here services one, and an enabled interrupt with no handler is a latch nobody
clears, the same call `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` made for TV_GINT0), `ports` (the mixer binding's
required output edge, which sun8i_mixer_of_get_id() derives the mixer index
from), and `assigned-clock-parents`, which the clock core applies before probe.

⚠ TWO CONSTRAINTS ON THE CLOCK, AND THE FIRST ONE CAN MOVE THE WHOLE SoC.

  1. THIS DRIVER DOES NOT CALL clk_set_rate() ON THE MODULE CLOCK, on purpose.
     DE0_CLK's source mux is DEPLL3X / DEPLL4X / PERI0_480M /
     PERI0_400M / PERI0_300M / VIDEO0PLL4X / VIDEO2PLL4X, and UM 4.1.4.3
     (A733 user manual) advises against changing DEPLL3X, because it also
     feeds NSI, the SoC interconnect, at 700 MHz. A
     set_rate here that propagated to that parent would move the fabric clock
     under every other block on the chip to suit a display mode. ``clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset`` reaches
     the same conclusion from the CCU side and gives de0 CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT
     with no CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT anywhere in the chain, so the two halves agree
     without either having to trust the other.
     The reset value closes on the UM's own number, which is the cross-check on
     the field decode: PLL_DE_CTRL_REG reset 0x40125600 gives N=87, M1=1, P1=3
     over the 24 MHz PLL_REF, so DEPLL3X = 24 x 87 / 3 = 696 MHz -- "696MHz by
     default", verbatim.
     ⚠ VIDEO1PLL4X is ABSENT from that mux, and VIDEO1PLL4X is the net `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`
     retunes to 308 MHz for the 154 MHz pixel clock. The DE and the eDP pixel
     path cannot contend for the same PLL.
  2. The driver READS the rate back, refuses below the rate this mode fetches
     at (154 MHz), and logs what it got. It assumes NOTHING about which parent
     it is given -- see the next block, which is why.

⚠ WHICH PARENT THE DE ACTUALLY RUNS FROM, MEASURED -- AND A CLAIM THIS HEADER
USED TO MAKE AND NO LONGER DOES.

Read on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-19 with the vendor's display engine GENUINELY LIVE (DE
version 0x03520100, VI scanout 0x05101018 = 0xFC000000, picture on the monitor):

    PLL_DE_CTRL   0x020022E0 = 0xFD124A00   bit31 SET -- PLL_DE is ON
    DE0_CLK       0x02002A00 = 0x80000000   gate on, mux [26:24] = 000 =
                                            DEPLL3X, FACTOR_M [4:0] = 0 -> M = 1
    DEPLL gates   0x02003928 = 0x00030000   bits 17:16, the DE PLL tap gates

So the vendor's working DE runs from DEPLL3X -- the RESET DEFAULT of that mux --
not from a PERI0 tap. Decoding 0x??124A00 with the field map below: N field
0x4A -> N = 75, M1 = 1, P1 = 3, so the VCO is 24 x 75 = 1800 MHz and DEPLL3X is
600 MHz. Worth noticing twice: 600 MHz is also exactly the mod_rate mainline's
DE33 config carries for the H616, reached from the other direction.

That field map is cross-checked against the UM's own statement rather than
assumed: PLL_DE_CTRL_REG's RESET value 0x40125600 decodes under it as N = 87,
M1 = 1, P1 = 3, i.e. DEPLL3X = 24 x 87 / 3 = 696 MHz -- "696MHz by default",
verbatim. One map, two values, each landing on its own documented or measured
number.

⛔ DELETED, NOT QUALIFIED: an earlier version of this header said PLL_DE is off
on this board and that leaving the mux alone yields a stopped clock. It rested
on a 2026-07-18 reading taken on a board whose DE was DOWN, where the PLL was
naturally down with it. A reading of an idle block is not evidence about a
running one, and the corrected reading is above.

WHAT THAT CHANGES FOR THIS DRIVER: nothing in its register set, and one thing in
its posture. `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-node-and-de-to-tcon-edge` pins PERI0_480M; the vendor uses DEPLL3X at 600 MHz; both
clear this mode's 154 MHz floor. THIS DRIVER IS WRITTEN FOR NEITHER. It never
sets a rate, reads back what it was given, refuses loudly below the floor, and
prints the rate at enable so a board session RECORDS which parent was in effect
rather than inheriting an assumption about it.

⚠ AND THE FAILURE PATH NAMES ADDRESSES, BECAUSE FOUR FAULTS READ ALIKE. A gated
block on this SoC answers 0x00000000 without faulting, so a zero cannot tell a
wrong base from a reset still asserted from a shut gate from a source clock that
is not running -- and clk_get_rate() cannot settle it either, since a sunxi PLL
recalc computes from its register fields whether or not PLL_EN is set, so a
stopped PLL still reports a plausible rate. So the rate is printed as evidence,
never as a verdict, and sun60i_de_check_alive() prints all four addresses with
the values a live vendor DE carries:

    DE0_BGR      0x02002A04   expect 0x00010001
    DE0_CLK      0x02002A00   bit31 gate, [26:24] mux, [4:0] M   (live 0x80000000)
    PLL_DE_CTRL  0x020022E0   bit31 PLL_EN                       (live 0xFD124A00)
    DE tap gates 0x02003928   bits 17:16                         (live 0x00030000)

The last two matter only when the mux selects a DE PLL tap -- which is what the
vendor's own live DE does -- so they are named either way rather than omitted on
the assumption that the device tree pinned a PERI0 parent.

This is the practice that earned its keep on 2026-08-19: `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` was correct and
still produced a dead raster because `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` withheld a reg window and a clock, and
it was diagnosed in ten minutes only because the driver announced the gap WITH
THE ADDRESSES. Every reg window, clock and reset this driver expects is named
the same way -- the windows at probe, the clocks here.

Nothing else is owed to the CCU: DE_SYS_BGR (0x0A74), the AHB matrix gate
(0x05C0 bit 5), the MBUS matrix gate (0x05E0 bit 11 -- the path both the
scanout fetch and any RCQ descriptor fetch use) and CM_DESYS_CFG (0x1B04) are
all in their working state at power-on and the vendor leaves all four alone.

NUMBERING
---------

configs/a733_defconfig gains CONFIG_DRM_SUN60I_DE=m. That file IS the build's
input, and scripts/check-defconfig.sh requires it to
round-trip through savedefconfig -- so the line sits exactly where Kbuild puts
it, and the round-trip was re-run locally until clean rather than pushed and
watched. scripts/build.sh gains DRM_SUN60I_DE in A733_MODULES, the =m assertion
in the compile gate, and sun60i-de.ko in A733_MODULE_FILES. That last one is the list
that has been short three times; `nm -u` over the built object lists 42
undefined symbols and not one is from drm.ko, so it adds nothing to the
closure -- a statement about its symbol table, not depmod's opinion.

