8. 🔴 THE BLACK SCREEN, IF IT IS STILL BLACK -- one write, two readings.

     cat   /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/state    # the three CCSC lines
     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/ccsc  # disable the block instead

   PASS (either way): the picture appears. If it appears with ccsc = 1 and the
   coefficients reading 0x00020000, the vendor's configuration is right and this
   patch is complete. If it takes ccsc = 0, the A523/T527 reading is right for
   this SoC and the default flips in a one-line follow-up.
   FALSIFIER: black under BOTH. Then the CCSC is not on the path, this patch's
   leading hypothesis is wrong, and the bisect moves to
   the capture-diff entry at 0x05008030 --
   ⚠ ONE GROUP AT A TIME. Thirty deltas in one batch is what lost the
   attribution on 2026-08-19 and wedged the board.

9. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL.

     echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/enable   # picture goes
     echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5100000.mixer/enable   # picture back

   PASS: the screen loses the picture and gets it back. A second enable landing
   correctly also exercises the disarm and the stop-before-programming path,
   which a first enable off a fresh reset cannot.

PARTLY HARDWARE-PROVEN, and the parts are separated on purpose.
HW-PROVEN (the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-19): the register set, the mode, the
format, the direct-MMIO commit path on a cold DE, the fetch of this driver's own
buffer at its own physical address, and zero data-supply errors.
HW-PROVEN: sun60i_de_top_open() and sun60i_de_verify() -- one
`enable`, nothing typed after it, whole datapath programmed, no error.
NOT PROVEN: the CCSC unity matrix in this revision, which is a derivation from
the capture and has not been on a board; and the gradient/border geometry test,
which has still never been looked at. Applies at --fuzz=0 on the full series;
drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ builds W=1 warning-free with CONFIG_DRM_SUN60I_DE=m
cross-compiled for arm64; checkpatch --strict reports 0 errors, 0 warnings,
0 checks over 1823 lines.

CORRECTED 2026-09-22, comments only. Two comments said the layer
address register is 32 bits wide and no VI high-address register exists. It
exists: VI +0x0d0..+0x0e4 (TOP_HADDR0-2, BOT_HADDR0-2) carry one byte per
layer, and a 1 in layer 1's byte moved that layer's fetch 4 GiB up (DP sink
CRC with a control, 8 GB board).
The search that found nothing read 0x05101080, which is layer
2's plane-2 address. So probe()'s 32-bit DMA mask is this driver's choice --
it writes no high-address byte -- not a hardware limit. No code changed: the
refusal string "... the layer address register is 32 bits" still gives the old
reason and is corrected with the next code change of this file.

